## INTRODUCTION
Hydrogen is a possible clean and renewable alternative for fossil fuels in the future.1 Electrocatalytic reduction of water to molecular hydrogen via hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) may provide a simple but efficient solution to future energy demands. To attain high current density at low overpotential for practical applications, the HER always requires efficient electrocatalysts owing to the multi-electron nature of dihydrogen generation through proton reduction.2 Pioneering studies have demonstrated that $\mathrm{Pt}$ -group metals are currently the best HER catalysts,3 however, their widespread practical utilization were hampered by the prohibitive cost and low abundance of these precious metals. As a consequence, the exploitation of inexpensive and effective catalysts is highly desirable to viable water electrolytic systems. In addition, the strongly acidic media in proton-exchange membrane (PEM) technology need acid-stable catalysts for PEM-based electrolysis units.4 Tremendous efforts have thus been devoted to developing acid-stable non-noble-metal catalysts to improve their HER activity, and encouraging progress has been achieved in the past few years.5
One of the exciting families of such catalysts, molybdenum-based materials including $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{Mo}}_{2}{\mathrm{C}},^{6}$ has been a great success owing to their similar electronic structures to that of noble metals. Moreover, catalysts with permanent porosity possessing larger surface areas are favorable for enhanced HER activity.7 Therefore, the design and preparation of active, selective, environmentally benign, and recyclable porous non-noble-metalbased catalysts toward HER is expected to have a significant impact on practical applications.
Polyoxometalate (POM) ions, which represent a welldefined library of inorganic building blocks with nanoscale size and oxygen-rich surface, are ideal candidates for the design and construction of tailored framework materials.8 Additionally, POMs with redox activity show great promise as redox catalysts for many organic transformations.9 Nevertheless, the applications of pure bulk POMs as solid catalysts are limited because of their significant solubility in reaction media, which leads to poor recyclability due to the disintegration of active sites. To improve the recyclability, POMs were immobilized on Lewis acidic porous supports (such as zeolites).9a,b However, these materials often suffer from difficulties in synthetic control and leaching of POMs from the porous support.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a new class of porous materials with fascinating structures and intriguing properties.10 Although MOFs with desirable proton conductivity were reported, they have rarely been studied as electrocatalysts owing to their low electrical conductivity. However, the permanent porosity and high surface area of MOFs may provide an advantage toward electrocatalytic reactions11 such as HER and ORR (oxygen reduction reaction). The availability of various building blocks consisting of POM anions and organic linkers (or metalorganic fragments) makes it possible to construct POMbased MOF materials. Thus, combining the advantages of both POMs and MOFs, such materials may exhibit excellent catalytic activity as electrocatalysts or heterogeneous catalysts. Though POM-based MOF materials were extensively studied as catalysts,12 so far, there is no report on POM-based MOF materials utilized as HER catalysts.
POM-based MOFs combine the redox nature of the POM moiety and the porosity of a MOF structure, which may favor hydrogen generation as an electrocatalyst. In addition, the stability of HER catalysts in water over a wide range of pH values is critical. Therefore, it is an important and challenging task to explore the appropriate POMs and suitable organic linkers for the preparation of stable crystalline porous POM-based MOF materials as non-precious-metal-based electrocatalysts.
Various POM-based MOF crystalline materials were reported in the literatures, and we classified them into three main forms in our recent review (Figure S1).12 In the light of the characters of HER catalysts, we employ $\varepsilon$ -Keggin polymolybdate units capped by four M metallic ions as secondary building blocks $\bf\Tilde{M}=\bf Z n^{\mathrm{II}}$ or $\mathrm{La}^{\mathrm{III}}.$ ) to obtain MOFs in which POM units and organic fragments were directly connected.13 Particularly, $\mathrm{\{\varepsilon-PMo^{V}{}_{8}M o^{V I}{}_{4}O_{40}Z n_{4}\}}$ (Zn-ε-Keggin, Figure 1a) stands out for the following considerations: (i) the Zn-ε-Keggin fragment can be generated in situ under mild conditions, and owns excellent redox activity on the basis of the Mo element; and (ii) The four exposed $\mathrm{Zn}^{\mathrm{II}}$ cations are in a regular tetrahedral arrangement, which is identical to those of O atoms in a tetrahedral $\mathrm{SiO}_{4}$ unit. This arrangement tends to promote the formation of $^4$ -connected $\mathrm{3D}$ MOFs due to its intrinsic nonplanarity.10d Recently, we devoted our efforts to isolating a Zn-ε-Keggin fragment, and fortunately, we successfully obtain a $\{[\varepsilon\mathrm{-PMo}^{\vee}\mathrm{_{8}M o}^{\vee\mathrm{I}}{}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{37}(\mathrm{OH})_{3}\mathrm{Zn}_{4}]\mathrm{Cl}_{4}\}^{4-}$ unit (noted as Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl), as crystallized in $\mathrm{\small{[TBA]}_{4}[\varepsilon-P M o^{V}_{8}M o^{V I}_{4}O_{37}(O\bar{H})_{3}Z n_{4}]C l_{4}}$ (NENU-499, $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ tetrabutylammonium ion). Based on this, we inferred there are two steps in the one-pot routine: i) the Zn-ε- Keggin-Cl was firstly formed and ii) Cl was substituted by the carboxylate group of organic ligands. The replacement reaction is supported by DFT calculations. The binding energy $(\Delta\mathrm{E}_{b})$ of POM-based framework was evaluated with chloride or carboxylate group, as shown in Figure $S_{2}$ . The calculated results suggest that the stability enhancement in the presence of carboxylate is attributable to the larger $\Delta\mathrm{E}_{b}$ value (see the Supporting Information, Table $\mathrm{S1}$ ). Bearing this in mind, rigid benzene tribenzoate $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB)}^{14}$ and $\big[\mathbf{1},\mathbf{1}^{'}$ -biphenyl]-3,4',5-tricarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BPT})^{15}$ were selected to put the theory to the test. Fortunately, two novel 3D framework, [TBA]3[ε-PMoV8MoVI4 $\mathrm{O_{36}(O H)_{4}Z n_{4}][C_{24}H_{15}(C O O)_{3}]_{4/3}\cdot18}$ $_{\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}$ (NENU-500) and $\mathrm{[\bar{TBA}]_{3}[\varepsilon-P M o^{V}_{8}M o^{V1}_{4}O_{37}(O H)_{3}Z n_{4}][B P T]}$ (NENU-501), were isolated and characterized (Figures $S_{3}$ and $S_{4}$ ). Furthermore, the electrocatalytic activities of the assynthesized NENU-500 and NENU-501 toward HER were examined in acidic aqueous solution (0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\cal O}_{4})$ ). As a novel non-noble metal HER catalyst in acidic media, NENU-500 is highly active with a Tafel slope of 96 $\mathrm{{m}V\cdot d e c^{-1}}$ and an exchange current density of 0.139 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . And it needs overpotentials $(\eta)$ of $327~\mathrm{mV}$ to attain current densities of 10 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . In addition, the free energy of Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl unit was studied to evaluate their HER activity by DFT calculations. NENU-500 and NENU-501 maintains their activities after 2000 cycles. Meanwhile, the HER behaviors of commercial $\mathrm{Pt/C_{:}}$ , $\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3},$ $\mathbf{NENU-5}$ , and HKUST-1 were also examined as a comparison.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
NENU-499. Single-crystal X-ray analysis demonstrates that NENU-499 crystallizes in the tetragonal $P-42_{1}c$ space group (Table S2). NENU-499 consists of Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl unit and $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ cations (Figure $S_{5}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ). The $\varepsilon{\mathrm{-PMo}}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ core, first reported by Sécheresse and co-workers,13 derives formally from the $\upalpha$ -Keggin isomer by rotation of all four $\mathrm{Mo}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{13}$ groups by $60^{\underline{{\mathbf{o}}}}$ around the $C_{3}$ axes (Figure S6). Up to now, only ε-Keggin ions capped by ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ , $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and ${\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}}$ were always encountered in the literatures,16 but the naked ε-Keggin has never been isolated, which perhaps results from the high negative charge numbers of ε-Keggin ions. As expected, ε-Keggin ion is a eight electronreduced POM unit, containing eight $\mathrm{{Mo}(V)}$ and four $\mathbf{Mo}(\mathrm{VI})$ ions, as always evidenced by the short $\mathrm{{\calMo}(V)}$ - $\mathrm{{\mathbf{Mo}(V)}}$ bonds $(-2.6\ \mathring\mathrm{A})$ and long $\mathrm{Mo(VI)-Mo(VI)}$ bonds $({\bf\underline{{\ }}}{}_{1}{}_{2}{}_{.}{}_{2}{}\hat{\bf\underline{{A}}})$ . Meanwhile, it is clear to see from the bond va
