lence sum calculations on the Mo centers (Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ) as well as the XPS analysis (Figure $S_{7}$ ). As far as we know, it is the first time to prepare the Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl fragment. It is of great importance to isolate this Zn-ε-Keggin-based monomer. As mentioned by the DFT results, this monomer maybe occurred as an intermediate during the formation of POM-based MOFs. Under the guidance of DFT calculations, we successfully synthesized NENU-500 and NENU-501 by the modification of Zn-ε-Keggin with organic moieties.
NENU-500. Single-crystal $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray analysis reveals that NENU-500 crystallizes in the highest cubic space group of Ia-3d $\left({}_{230}\right)$ . The asymmetric unit contains one [ε- $\mathrm{PMo^{V}_{8}M o^{V I}_{4}O_{36}(O H)_{4}Z n_{4}]^{+}}$ ion (Figure S8), $\mathrm{BTB^{3^{-}}}$ fragment with $_{4/3}$ occupancy factor, three $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ cations and eighteen $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecules. The electron of ε-Keggin unit in NENU-500 is highly delocalized owing to the high symmetry, as confirmed by the bond valence sum calculations (Table $S_{3}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ) as well as the XPS analysis (Figure S9). NENU500 is dark red (Figure S10), which is the typical color of reduced molybdenum blue species.17 Each Zn-ε-Keggin fragment, as regular inorganic quadridentate ligand, was connected by four $\mathrm{BTB}^{3-}$ linkers to be a $^4$ -connected node; in turn, each $\mathrm{BTB}^{3^{-}}$ were linked by three Zn-ε-Keggin units be a $^3$ -connected linker (Figure S11). Such connectivity leads to a $\mathrm{3D}$ porous extended framework with a typical ctn topology (Figure 1b).18 To the best of our knowledge, only a few frameworks with ctn topology have been documented,19 however, POM-based MOF with ctn topology was rarely reported. Nature abhors a vacuum. In order to minimize the big void cavities and stabilize the framework, the single network allows another identical network to penetrate it in a staggered mode, thus resulting in two-fold interpenetrating ctn arrays (Figure 1c).20 In Dolbecq’s previous work,21 1,3,5- benzenetricarboxylic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{3}\mathrm{BTC)}$ was employed as linkers to connect with tetrahedral Zn-ε-Keggin moiety, resulting in a $\mathrm{3D}$ open-framework POM-based compound (noted as $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3})$ with ofp topology. In the two compounds, though $\mathrm{BTB}^{3^{-}}$ and $\mathsf{B T C}^{3^{-}}$ both play the role of tridentate linkers and Zn-ε-Keggin as a tetrahedral node, the final products own two different types of topology. The only notable difference between the two structure types perhaps can be attributed to the different point symmetry of $^3$ -coordinated node.19b X-Ray diffraction analysis indicates that 1D channel can be observed running parallel to [111] direction in NENU-500, enclosed in the channel are disordered guest water molecules and bulky $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ countercations. However, the positions of $_\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ molecules and $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions could not be determined by X-ray diffraction due to the severe disorder. $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions are served as structure-directing agents, space-filling agents and charge-compensating cations.
With the substitution of $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTB}$ by $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBPT}$ under the similar condition, NENU-501 was yielded (Figure S12). NENU-501 crystallizes in the monoclinic space group $C_{2}/c$ (15). The asymmetric unit consists of one neutral {ε- $\mathrm{PMo}^{\mathrm{V}}{}_{8}\mathrm{\bar{M}o}^{\mathrm{VI}}{}_{4}\mathrm{O}_{37}(\mathrm{\bar{O}H)}_{3}\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\}$ fragment (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{13}\mathrm{\dot{}}$ ), one $\mathsf{B P T^{3^{-}}}$ moiety and three $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions. In NENU-501, the building block was not the expected Zn-ε-Keggin but a dimerized form of $Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{-}\varepsilon$ -Keggin as reported in the literature (Figure 2).21 The dimeric Zn-ε-Keggin unit perhaps results from the aggregation of monomeric Zn-ε-Keggin unit. The dimeric Zn-ε-Keggin fragment has six anchoring points, exhibiting the staggered conformation of ethane. The $\mathrm{BPT^{3^{-}}}$ units bridged three dimeric fragments to give rise to a $\mathrm{3D}$ (3,6)-connected network with flu- $_{-3,6-C_{2}/c}$ topology (Figures 2 and $\mathrm{S14}$ ).22 Due to the dimerization, the ratio of $Z\mathrm{n}\mathrm{-}\varepsilon$ -Keggin to organic linker is 1 instead of $4/3$ in NENU-500. As in NENU-500, the charge of the anionic framework is compensated by $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ cations located in the channels of NENU-501.
The phase purities of NENU-499 − NENU-501 were established by comparison of their observed and simulated X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD) patterns (Figures S15 and S16). NENU-500 and NENU-501 show good thermal stabilities with decomposition starting at 300 and $366^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. NENU-500 exhibits a continuous weight loss step from $300$ to $560~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , corresponding to the loss of all water molecules, organic ligands and $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions. NENU500 exhibits a continuous weight loss step from $366$ to $554~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , corresponding to the loss of all organic ligands and $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions. The residue corresponds to $\mathrm{znO}$ and $\mathbf{MoO}_{3}$ . Furthermore, they are air-stable with the maintenance of their crystallinities for at least several months, and no efflorescence was observed. Additionally, NENU500 and NENU-501 are insoluble in common organic solvents, such as dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO), chloroform, ethanol, and acetone. Surprisingly, it was also found that they are stable in acidic and basic aqueous solution in the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of $_{1-12}$ at room temperature, as confirmed by the subsequent XRPD and FT/IR measurements (Figures $_3$ $\mathrm{S17}$ and S18). To the best of our knowledge, only a few examples of MOFs that are stable in both acidic and basic solutions have been reported,23 especially POM-based compounds that are often sensitive to the presence of $\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ions. This is essential to be used as HER catalysts for this kind of materials.
After the activation of the as-synthesized materials by immersing NENU-500 in methanol for 3 days and then heating at 100 $^{\circ}C$ in a vacuum overnight, it retained its crystallinity as confirmed by XRPD (Figure S19). The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms at $_{77}\mathrm{~K~}$ of the activated sample NENU-500a reveals a characteristic type I behavior for microporous materials with a slight hysteresis between the adsorption and desorption (Figure S20), which can be explained by the dynamic feature of the framework.24 The maximum $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption amount was 100.0 $\mathbf{cm}^{3}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at standard temperature and pressure (STP). The BrunauerEmmett-Teller (BET) area was calculated to be $\mathbf{195\m^{2}{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ based on the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption data. The BET area was not very high, which perhaps can be assigned to partial exchange of $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions. However, it is still higher than other metal nanomaterials as electrocatalysts.25 The architectural stability and permanent porosity of NENU-500 was confirmed by the $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption experiments. NENU-501 was treated under the same condition; however, little $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption was detected. This perhaps can be assigned to the very small pore of NENU-501 in which the $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ ions were not removed from the interspace. To further study the adsorption property, the water adsorption isotherm for NENU-500 was measured at 293 K. As shown in Figure S21, the amount of water uptake increases with a $P/P_{\mathrm{o}}$ increase and reaches $66.9~{\mathrm{cm}}^{3}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}\left(2.99~{\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}\right)$ at $P/P_{0}=$ 0.99.
Owing to their strong tolerance to both acid and basic media and perfect electrical conductivity, carbon materials with various structures are widely used as skeletons to support host materials, resulting in advanced materials for electrocatalysis and other energy-related applications.26 Therefore, we prepare the composite catalysts based on the powder samples of the as-synthesized materials and carbon black (Vulcan $x{\mathrm{C}}{\mathrm{-}}72{\mathrm{R}}$ ). The electrochemical behaviors of NENU-499 − NENU-501-carbon black (Vulcan $\begin{array}{r}{\operatorname{XC-}72\mathrm{R},}\end{array}$ ) on glassy carbon electrode (GCE) were carried out,27 as well as the electrocatalytic activities of NENU-500 and NENU-501 toward ${\mathrm{NO}}_{2}^{-}$ ions. NENU-499 exhibits three redox peaks in $\mathrm{0.1~mol{\cdot}L^{-1}~H_{2}S O_{4}}$ aqueous solution, and the approximate proportionality of the peak II-II' currents to the scan rates from 25 to 200 $\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ suggests that the redox process is surface-controlled for NENU-499-GCE (Figure $S22_{,}$ ). Figure $^4$ describes the typical CVs at a potential ranging from $-0.16$ to $+0.45^{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (vs. $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl)}$ in $\mathrm{0.1~mol{\cdot}L^{-1}~H_{2}S O_{4}}$ aqueous solution at different scan rates for NENU-500 and NENU-501-GCE. The mean peak potentials $\mathrm{E}_{1/2}\ =\ (\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{pa}}{+}\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{pc}})/2$ for NENU-500 appeared at about −0.091 (I-I'), $+0.176$ (II-II') and $+0.296\mathrm{V}$ (III-III') with peak potential separations of 35, 13 and 23 $\mathrm{mV}$ (scan rate: $50\ \mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathbf{s}^{-1})$ , respectively. The mean peak potentials for NENU-501 appeared at about −0.092 (I-I'), $+\mathbf{0.177}$ (II-II') and $+0.288$ V (III-III') with peak potential separations of 26, 12 and $22~\mathrm{mV}$ , respectively (scan rate: 50 $\mathrm{mV}\cdot\mathbf{s}^{-1})$ , respectively. These data were similar to the reported literature.28 The approximate proportionality of the three redox peak currents to the scan rates from 25 to
