Tafel slope is an inherent property of electrocatalytic material, which is determined by the rate-limiting step of HER. Additionally, the determination and interpretation of Tafel slope is of importance for the elucidation of HER mechanism involved. Figure $5\mathrm{b}$ displays the Tafel plots for NENU-499 − NENU-501. Meanwhile, the Tafel plots for $\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3},$ NENU- $\cdot5,$ , HKUST- $\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{\l}$ , $X C-72R$ , and $\mathrm{Pt}/C$ are also presented for comparison. The linear portions of the Tafel plots are fitted to the Tafel equation $(\eta\ =\ b\ \log|j|\ +\ a,$ , where $j$ is the current density, $b$ is the Tafel slope, and $a$ is the intercept relative to the exchange current density $j_{\mathrm{o,}}^{\mathrm{~~}}$ ). Commercial $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}$ shows the Tafel slope of ${\sim}30~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ that is in consistent with the reported value,32 confirming the validity of our electrochemical measurements. The Tafel slopes of NENU- $\mathbf{500}$ , NENU- $\mathbf{\sigma}_{\cdot501}$ , $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3},$ NENU499, NENU- ${\bf5}$ and HKUST-1 yields from the Tafel plots are 96, 137, 142, 122, 94, and 127 $\mathrm{{mV}\cdot d e c^{-1}}$ , respectively. The exchange current density $(j_{\mathrm{o}})$ of NENU-500 is calculated to be about 0.139 $\mathbf{mA}{\cdot}\mathbf{cm}^{-2}$ . Compared with those non-noble electrocatalytic materials reported recently, NENU-500 electrode presents a quite large exchange current density and a relatively small Tafel slope,33 suggesting NENU-500 is a promising low-cost and earth-abundant metallic electrocatalyst for HER.
A promising material for electrocatalytic HER should exhibit not only high activity but also good durability. Therefore, we also examined the stability of NENU-500 and NENU-501 in hydrogen generation by accelerated degradation experiment. As shown in Figure S29, after continuous CV scanning for 2000 cycles in 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ aqueous solution at a scan rate of 100 $\mathrm{mV}{\cdot}\mathbf{s}^{-1}.$ , the polarization curves show negligible difference compared with the initial one. The result demonstrates that NENU-500 and NENU-501 modified electrodes are durable during electrocatalytic hydrogen production. Furthermore, their stabilities in 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ aqueous solution were also evaluated by immersing as-synthesized NENU-500 and NENU501 in acidic solution for 6 hours. From the XRPD patterns, it can be clearly seen that the peaks keep the positions (Figure S16), suggesting the maintenance of the frameworks. In addition, the XRPD patterns of $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3},$ NENU- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf5}$ and HKUST-1 treated under the same conditions cannot keep the peak positions (Figure $\mathrm{S}_{30}\mathrm{\overline{{\Omega}}}$ ), indicating they are not stable in 0.5 M ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ aqueous solution. Especially, the color and appearance of HKUST-1 immediately changed once soaked in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ aqueous solution. The results reveal that NENU-500 and NENU-501 have superior stability in a long-term electrochemical process compared to $\varepsilon(\mathrm{trim})_{4/3},$ NENU-5 and HKUST-1.
To get further insight into the activity of as-synthesized NENU-500 and NENU-501 modified electrodes toward HER, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) analysis was also performed. Figures 6a and $\mathrm{S}_{3^{1}}$ describe the obtained Nyquist plots of NENU-500 and NENU-501, and the magnified Nyquist plots in high frequency region are presented for clarity (upper inset). The data were fitted to an equivalent circuit (Figures $\left.\mathrm{S}_{3}\right\mathbf{i}_{\mathrm{,}}$ ), and the resultant fitting parameters are summarized in Table S4. The charge-transfer resistance $(R_{\mathrm{ct}})$ at the surface of the catalysts is determined from the diameter of semicircle at high frequencies in Nyquist plot. Generally, $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value varies inversely to the electrocatalytic activity. That is, smaller diameter corresponds to faster HER kinetics. The $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of NENU-500 $(28~\Omega)$ and NENU-501 $(58~\Omega)$ measured at $-550~\mathrm{mV}$ (vs. $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}\mathrm{\dot{\Omega}}$ ) is much lower than those of $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3}$ (3309 $\Omega^{\cdot}$ ), NENU- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf5}$ $\left(2381~\Omega\right)$ and HKUST-1 $\left(1253~\Omega\right)$ (Figure 6b). Thus, such a low $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ value of NENU500 indicates that its high electrocatalytic activity for HER could be ascribed to the highly conductive carbon black hybrid by improving the charge transfer characteristics of NENU-500.
As suggested by Nørskov et al.,34 a good hydrogen evolution catalyst should have a free energy of adsorbed H close to that of the reactant or product (i. e., $\Delta G_{\mathrm{H}}^{\mathbf{o}}\approx\mathbf{o})$ ), which can provide a fast proton/electron-transfer step as well as a fast hydrogen release processes. To get a deeper understanding, we select Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl fragment as model to compute the free energy change for H adsorption on by means of DFT computations, owing to the presence of Zn-ε-Keggin unit in both NENU-500 and
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
NENU-501. As shown in Figure $S_{32}$ , there are three possible H adsorption sites on Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl, namely $\mu_{3}-$ 一 bridging oxygen $\mathrm{(O_{a})}$ , $\mu_{{}_{2}}$ -bridging oxygen $\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{b}}\right)$ , and terminal oxygen $\mathrm{(O_{t})}$ . According to our computations, $\mathrm{{O_{a}}}$ site is more favorable for $\mathrm{~H~}$ adsorption with its adsorption energy $(-\mathbf{o}.56\ \mathrm{eV})$ lower than those of $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{b}}$ site $(0.22~\mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{t}}$ site $(\mathbf{o}.52\ \mathrm{eV})$ , respectively. By taking amount of entropy and zero point energy, the free energy change for H adsorption on $\mathrm{{O_{a}}}$ site is computed to be $-0.07\ \mathrm{eV}$ , indicating that Zn-ε-Keggin-Cl truly has a good catalytic capacity for hydrogen evolution. This is one of the reasons that NENU-500 and NENU- ${\bf501}$ possess higher activity than other MOF materials.
NENU-500 exhibits the highest HER activity among NENU- $\mathbf{501}$ , $\varepsilon(\operatorname{trim})_{4/3},$ NENU-499, NENU- ${\bf5}$ and HKUST1, which perhaps can be assigned to the following reasons: (i) The poor HER activity of HKUST-1 below another five polymolybdate-based catalysts can be assigned to that it does not contain active sites of molybdophosphate fragment and its poor stability in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ aqueous solution. (ii) The lower HER activity of NENU- ${\bf5}$ than NENU500, NENU-501 and $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3}$ perhaps can be attributed to the structural factor.12 In NENU- ${\bf\cdot}{\bf5}$ , $\mathrm{\alpha{\alpha}\mathrm{-}P M o_{\mathrm{12}}O_{\mathrm{40}}}$ unit works as template located in the channel, while ε-Keggin moiety serves as node to directly connect organic ligands leading to porous MOFs and thus more active sites are exposed. On the other hand, NENU- $^{\cdot}{\boldsymbol{5}}$ was also unstable in 0.5 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}$ aqueous solution. (iii) The HER activity of
NENU-499 suggests that Zn-ε-Keggin fragment contributes to the lower overpotential. However, the rigid and stable nature of NENU-500, NENU-501 and $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3}$ favors the period of porous structures, which perhaps allows an easy diffusion of electrolyte into the pore and thus a more efficient utilization of active sites than in NENU-499. (iv) The HER activity of NENU-500 is better than those of NENU-501 and $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3}$ perhaps resulting from porous factor. NENU-501 and $\pmb{\varepsilon}(\mathbf{trim})_{4/3}$ hardly have any adsorption toward nitrogen. Furthermore, NENU-500 has a much lower $R_{\mathrm{ct}}$ than those of NENU-501 and $\varepsilon(\operatorname{trim})_{4/3},$ which results in more facile electrode kinetics.35 As a result, the stable porous POM-based MOF with POM node directly connecting with organic linker exhibits the highest HER activity as a electrocatalyst, which will be a new promising branch of POM-based MOF materials as HER catalysts.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, two POM-based MOF materials, NENU500 and NENU-501, were successfully isolated, in which POM fragments work as nodes to directly connect with organic ligands. In addition, NENU-500 represents a rare example of $\ensuremath{3\mathrm{D}}$ porous MOF with a typical ctn network, especially as a POM-based MOF. NENU-500 and NENU501 exhibit not only good air-stability but also tolerance to acid and base media. Therefore, NENU-500 and NENU-501, as POM-based MOFs, were utilized as electrocatalysts toward HER for the first time, owing to the combination of redox nature of POM moieties and porosity of MOF structure. Remarkably, NENU-500 is highly active for electrochemically generating hydrogen from acidic water with a Tafel slope of $96~\mathrm{mV\cdotdec^{-1}}$ and an exchange current density of 0.139 $\mathrm{\mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . It should be noted that NENU-500 exhibits the best HER performance among all MOF materials due to its good stability, porosity and exposed active sites. Furthermore, the HER activities of NENU-500 and NENU-501 were maintained after 2000 cycles. The facile preparation of these POM-based MOF materials, along with its long-term aqueous stability, low overpotential and high activity, offers promising features for potential use in hydrogen generation. The present study not only offers us a successful case to construct stable POM-based MOFs with high porosity, but provides us with novel hydrogen-evolving electrocatalysts with excellent activity. We are convinced that this newly emerging hydrogen-evolving electrocatalysts is bubbling with opportunities, and that significant progress will be made in the years ahead. More investigation is currently underway in our group.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Materials: All the chemicals were obtained from commercial sources, and were used without further purification. Deionized water was used for preparation of NENU499 and NENU-500, and ultrapure water (resistivity: ${\mathsf{\rho}}\geqslant$ $18~\mathrm{M}\Omega\cdot\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) was used in electrochemical experiments. IR spectrum was performed in the range 4000–400 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ using KBr pellets on an Alpha Centaurt FT/IR spectrophotometer. X-ray powder diffraction (XRPD) measurements were recorded ranging from $^3$ to $50^{\mathrm{{\underline{{{o}}}}}}$ at room temperature
## Journal of the American Chemical Society
on a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with $\mathrm{Cu-K}_{\alpha}$ $\langle\lambda=$ $\mathbf{1.5418\:\mathring{A}}_{,}^{\cdot}$ ). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) of the samples was performed using a Perkin-Elmer TG-7 analyzer heated from room temperature to 900 $^{\circ}C$ under nitrogen at the heating rate of $5\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . XPS analysis was performed on a thermo ECSALAB 250 spectrometer with an Al $\operatorname{K}\upalpha$ $(\mathrm{1486.6~eV})$ achromatic X-ray source running at 15 kV. The XPS binding energy (BE) was internally referenced to the aliphatic C(1s) peak (BE, $284.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ . Fieldemission scanning electron microscopy (FE SEM) images were obtained with a Hitachi SU-8010 electron microscope (Hitachi, Tokyo, Japan).
Preparation of NENU-499: A mixture of sodium molybdate dihydrate ( $\mathrm{618~mg}$ , 2.55 mmol), Mo powder $99.99\%$ (50 mg, 0.52 mmol), ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{3}$ $20\ \mathrm{mg},0.25\ \mathrm{mmol})$ , zinc chloride $\mathrm{^{136~mg}}$ , 1.00 mmol), tetrabutylammonium hydroxide $40~\mathrm{wt\%}$ solution in water (120 $\upmu\mathrm{L}$ , $\mathbf{o.18~mmol}_{.}$ ), and $H_{2}O$ (8 mL) was stirred for 20 min, and the $\mathsf{p H}$ was acidified to $_{4.8}$ with diluted HCl $\left(2\mathrm{~M~}\right)$ . Then, 6-nitrobenzimidazole (82 g, 0.50 mmol) was added to the mixture, which was transferred and sealed in a 15 mL Teflonlined stainless steel container, and heated at $\scriptstyle180^{\circ}C$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature at 10 $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ , dark red crystals suitable for $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction study were harvested.
Preparation of NENU-500 and NENU-501: A mixture of $\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{MoO}_{4}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{618~mg}$ , 2.55 mmol), molybdenum powder $99.99\%$ (50 mg, 0.52 mmol), ${\mathrm{H}}_{3}{\mathrm{PO}}_{3}$ (20 mg, 0.25 mmol), zinc chloride $\left(136\mathrm{\mg},1.00\mathrm{\mmol}\right),$ ), $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB}$ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}\mathrm{130~mg}}$ , 0.30 mmol), tetrabutylammonium hydroxide $40\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ solution in water ( ${\mathrm{120~\upmuL,}}$ , $\mathbf{0.18\mmol}$ ), and $H_{2}O$ $(7~\mathrm{mL})$ ) was stirred for 20 min, and the $\mathsf{p H}$ was acidified to $_{4.8}$ with diluted HCl $\left({}_{2}\mathrm{~M}\right)$ . Then, the mixture was transferred and sealed in a 15 mL Teflonlined stainless steel container, and heated at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooling to room temperature at 10 $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ , dark red crystals (NENU-500) suitable for $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray diffraction study were harvested (yield $68\%$ based on $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{BTB})$ ). IR (Figure $S_{3}$ , KBr pellets, $\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ): 3450 (s), 2961 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 1599 $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ , 1550 (w), 1466 (w), 1376 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 1110 (w), 935 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , $8\mathbf{1}_{4}$ $\mathrm{(m)}$ , $780$ $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 705 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , $587$ (m). NENU501 was isolated by an analogous method with NENU-500, only using $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBPT}$ in substitution for $\mathrm{\bfH}_{3}\mathrm{\bfBTB}$ . IR (Figure $S_{3}$ , KBr pellets, $\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ ): 3445 (w), 2961 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , $2872$ $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 1559 $\mathbf{\tau}(\mathbf{m})$ , 1470 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 1350 (s), 1148 (w), 942 (s), 819 (s), 777 (s), 707 $\mathrm{(m)}$ , 590 $(\mathbf{m})$ ), 486 (w).
Single Crystal X-ray Crystallography: Suitable single crystals were selected and mounted onto the end of a thin glass fiber using Fomblin oil. Single crystal X-ray diffraction data were recorded on a Bruker APEXІІ CCD diffractometer with graphite-monochromated Mo $\mathrm{K}_{\upalpha}$ radiation $(\lambda=0.7^{10}73\mathrm{\AA}),$ at 293 K. Absorption corrections were applied using multi-scan technique. The structure was solved by Direct Method of SHELXS- $97^{3^{6a}}$ and refined by full-matrix least-squares techniques using the SHELXL-97 program36b within WINGX.36c For NENU-500, the $\mathrm{TBA}^{+}$ cations could not be located in the structure due to severe disorder, and the data were corrected with SQUEEZE,37 a part of the PLATON package of crystallographic software used to calculate the solvent molecules or counterions disorder area and to remove the contribution to the overall intensity data. The crystallographic information is presented in Table S2.
Electrochemical Measurements: Cyclic voltammetry (CV) and linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) tests were conducted with a CHI830B workstation (CH Instruments, China) in a conventional three electrode system. A modified glassy carbon electrode (GCE, $d=3$ or $5~\mathrm{mm},$ ) was served as the working electrode in electrochemical experiments, a platinum wire as the counter electrode, and an $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode as the reference electrode, respectively. In addition, the GCE with diameter of $5\mathrm{mm}$ was used as rotating disk electrode (RDE, $\mathbf{0.19625~cm}^{2}$ , Princeton Applied Research Instrumentation, USA). Meanwhile, a speed control unit-Princeton Applied Research Model 616 Electrode Rotator was used for RDE measurements. In the identical cell setup, electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was carried out on a PARSTAT 2273 electrochemical system (Princeton Applied Research Instrumentation, USA). The frequency range covers from 10.0 kHz to 0.1 Hz with modulation amplitude of 10 $\mathrm{mV}$ at different bias voltages. Prior to measurement, the solution was bubbled with nitrogen gas for 30 min to remove dissolved oxygen. In the measurements, the $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}$ reference electrode was calculated with respect to reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) according to reported method.38 The formula is $\mathrm{E(RHE)=E(Ag/AgCl)+0.059pH+0.198V},$ where E(RHE) is a potential vs. RHE, $\mathrm{E(Ag/AgCl)}$ is a potential vs. $\mathrm{\sfAg/AgCl}$ electrode, and $\mathsf{p H}$ is the $\mathsf{p H}$ value of electrolyte. LSV measurements was carried out in $\mathbf{0.5\M}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ with a scan rate of $5\mathrm{mV^{\cdot}s^{-1}}$ , which were examined after 500 cycles of CV tests in the range of 0 to $-\mathbf{1}.2\mathrm{~V~}$ to stabilize the current. The working electrode was rotated at 1000 rpm to remove hydrogen gas bubbles formed at the catalyst surface. The durability tests were carried out by repeating the potential scan from 0 to $-\mathbf{1}~\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of 100 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ for 2000 cycles. All current densities are the ratios of currents and geometric areas of working electrodes. The EIS spectra were fitted by the Z-SimpWin software.
Preparation of Working Electrodes: A mixture of $20~\mathrm{mg}$ of carbon black (Vulcan $\tt X C-72R)$ and the desired amount of as-synthesized POM-based MOFs (20 or $5~\mathrm{mg}$ ) was cogrounded for 45 min. Prior to be modified, the GCE and RDE were polished carefully with ${\bf0.05\mu m}$ alumina powders and then cleaned with ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ (1:1), ethanol and deionized water, respectively. Catalyst ink was prepared by mixing $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the prepared catalyst powders into water $(950~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ containing ${\bf{0.5~w t\%}}$ Nafion $(50~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ , and then ultrasonically dispersed for 30 min. Then an aqueous dispersion was transferred onto the clean-washed GCE $(_{5}\upmu\mathrm{L})$ and RDE $\left(14.2~\upmu\mathrm{L}\right)$ , and dried in air at room temperature before electrochemical experiments.
